    fix(scheduler): SCHED-GAP-173 — status surface and enforcer share one failure classifier (harnessFailure)
    
    /api/v1/status's projects_failure_rates counted every failed/timeout row, while
    the auto-disable enforcer (CheckFailureRateAutoDisable) excludes harness-class
    failures via harnessFailure. A lane whose failures were all gateway noise
    therefore read failure_rate=0.91 / auto_disable_armed=true while the enforcer's
    own verdict on the same window was ~0.02 — live evidence 2026-09-19: heading,
    98 of its last 100 ticks "gateway unreachable ... HTTP 503". The enforcer was
    right; the read-only surface was lying, and PM/QA automation plus SCHED-GAP-170's
    per-lane-decay read inherited the lie.
    
    One authority, two surfaces:
    
    - internal/scheduler/failureclass.go (new): the marker list moves here as the
      exported HarnessFailure — the SINGLE source of truth the enforcer and the API
      surface both call. harnessFailure stays as an in-package forwarder to it, so
      failureReasonClass and the SCHED-GAP-134/143 tests keep working unchanged;
      the marker list itself is not duplicated and was not edited.
    - internal/api/server_helpers.go: computeProjectFailureRates now selects
      ticks.error alongside status and skips a row when
      status == "failed" && scheduler.HarnessFailure(err) — literally
      CheckFailureRateAutoDisable's predicate, including its failed-only gate (a
      TIMEOUT carrying gateway text still counts, because it counts for the
      enforcer). Excluded rows leave BOTH counters, so the surface's
      failure_rate is the ratio the enforcer compares against its threshold. A
      window that is entirely harness-class no longer drops out of the map
      (total 0, rate 0, armed false) — 0/0 would be NaN and break the JSON
      encoder, and silently vanishing from the surface would hide a
      harness-blocked lane — and armed stays false, which is the enforcer's
      verdict on that window.
    - internal/scheduler/alert_escalation.go: enforcer behavior, arithmetic and
      armed math untouched. Adds NewAlertEscalatorWithPolicy so the parity test
      can run the REAL enforcer (autoDisablePolicy is package-private).
    - internal/api/schedgap173_status_enforcer_parity_test.go: real
      GET /api/v1/status (httptest, temp DB) compared against the real
      CheckFailureRateAutoDisable on the same DB and the same policy numbers, with
      the enforcer's own counts (parsed out of disabled_reason) cross-checked
      against the surface's. Fixture: harness-only, mostly-noise-with-one-real-fault
      (1/40 = 0.025), project-faulted (5/10 = 0.5 → armed, the breaker must still
      fire), all-harness (total 0), clean, and timeout-with-harness-text. A second
      test pins the single-authority rule structurally: the marker list may exist
      in exactly one non-test file (failureclass.go), server_helpers.go must call
      scheduler.HarnessFailure and must not declare its own.
    
